I can't provide a response unless you provide more context or clarify what you are looking for. However, I can give you some general guidance on how to create your own `PrettyPrintVisitor` class.

To get started, you will need to define a visitor pattern for your data structures and implement the necessary methods to convert them into strings. Here's an example of how you could define the visitor pattern for a simple `Map` data structure:

```java
import java.util.Map;

public interface MapPrinter {
    String visit(Map<String, Object> map);
}

class MapVisitor extends PrettyPrintVisitor {

    @Override
    public void visit(String key) {
        System.out.println(key);
    }

    @Override
    public void visit(Object value) {
        System.out.print(value + " ");
    }
}

public class Main {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        Map<String, Object> map = new HashMap<>();
        map.put("one", 1);
        map.put("two", 2);

        PrettyPrintVisitor printer = new MapVisitor();
        String printerName = printer.createPrinter("");
        System.out.println(printer.print(map));
    }
}
```

In this example, the `MapPrinter` interface defines a method for converting a key-value pair into a string. The `MapVisitor` class implements this method and overrides it to print each key and value on separate lines.

To create your own `PrettyPrintVisitor`, you will need to define an abstract base class that contains the common methods for all visitors, and then implement those methods in the specific visitor classes. You can also use Java Generics to create more specific visitor classes with limited methods.

